+## What the verifier found
+
+The verifier refused to produce a verdict:
+
+- `only static methods are supported`
+
+## Would the existing tests have caught it?
+
+No: every existing unit test passes (`java -ea net.terminalvalue.crosscut.Tests`, run against the patched code).
+
+## Why it matters
+
+None: the proof is over every counter, clock reading and window length, the same result record or the same exception in both versions. What is at stake is the review of an arithmetic identity on 64-bit values, where a reader has to recall that Java's `/` truncates toward zero and `%` takes the dividend's sign, so that the identity holds for negative readings too (a clock reading from `System.nanoTime` can be negative).
+
+## Why the verdict is right
+
+The harness builds the counter from symbolic components, calls both versions on the same reading and window, and compares the result records component by component, or the exceptions by class. In the tool's own checker, `nowNanos / windowNanos` and `nowNanos % windowNanos` are one division left to the solver, with fresh bits for the quotient and remainder under what holds of every division; the product of that quotient and the divisor is not a multiplication at all but `nowNanos - remainder`, since `d == (d / p) * p + d % p` holds for every pair of values, so the new version's start is the old version's start, the same bits. The checker proves the harness in 0.1 s over 7 paths and 9 solver calls. JBMC, which has to relate a 64-bit divider to a 64-bit multiplier in one formula, did not finish within 60 s.
+
+## What to do
+
+Merge. The named index reads better and the proof says it is the same window.
+
+## Notes
+
+- No scope: the proof covers every input, the negative readings included, where both versions truncate toward zero (`-1 / 2 * 2` is 0, and `-1 - -1 % 2` is 0 too), and the inputs both versions refuse (a non-positive window, a reading before the start, a count that overflows into the constructor's check), where both throw `IllegalArgumentException`.
+- The tool's own checker rewrites a quotient times its divisor as the dividend less the remainder, `(a / b) * b` as `a - a % b`, exactly: the identity holds in two's complement for every pair, the division by zero (which the JVM throws on before the value matters) and `MIN_VALUE / -1` included. Both versions then compute the same bits for the start, and the comparison of the results folds to true before the solver is asked. The log (`bmc.log` in the work directory) says `0 of 0 multiplications built in full`: no product was left to the solver. Before the rewrite the checker did not finish this harness in two minutes, and example 133's last note records the same identity out of reach on the token bucket's credited time.
+- A product the identity does not cover is left to the solver, like a division: fresh bits under what holds of every product modulo the width (a factor of zero gives zero, of one the other factor, of minus one its negation, a power of two shifts, and the low eight bits are exact), with the multiplier built only where a model got a product wrong. Example 131's loop of `delay *= multiplier` on a multiplier of two went from 22 s to 4 s this way, none of its 27 products built, and example 139 is a product the facts settle by a factor of one.
+- JBMC, with its default solver, did not finish the same harness within 60 s: the identity over a 64-bit division and multiplication is where a SAT solver's unsatisfiability proof is slow, in JBMC and in this checker's circuits alike, which is why the checker does not build them for it. The reading `nowNanos / windowNanos * windowNanos` in one expression gives the same bits, the quotient being the same lazy division.
